# Paths the PR reviewer does not read (DRY-92).
#
# One extended regex per line, matched against the repository-relative path of
# every changed file. `#` comments and blank lines are ignored.
#
# What belongs here is REGENERATED ARTIFACTS, not merely large files. The test
# is: if this file is wrong, is the fix to edit it? For a generated file the
# answer is no — you re-run the generator — so a reviewer's comment on its diff
# has nowhere to land. A large hand-written file is exactly what the reviewer
# should be spending its budget on.
#
# THIS FILE IS READ FROM THE BASE BRANCH, never from the PR head, so a pull
# request cannot exempt itself from review by adding a line here. Editing it
# changes nothing until the PR merges — and because this path is in the
# SENSITIVE list in `pr-review.yml`, the PR proposing the edit gets the deeper
# pass rather than a lighter one.
#
# Note what is NOT here: `daemon/src/protocol.ts` and `shell/src/lib/protocol.ts`
# are duplicated verbatim, and it is tempting to read the second as a copy of
# the first. It isn't generated — nothing writes it — and the duplication is
# exactly the kind of thing that drifts, which is why `pr-checks.yml` diffs
# them. Both stay in scope.

# release-please writes this from the commit subjects. A hand-edit to it is the
# thing to fix, not the thing to review; the release PR that carries it is
# skipped at triage anyway, from the set derived from release-please-config.json.
^CHANGELOG\.md$

# The resolved dependency tree. Reviewing it line by line finds nothing anybody
# would act on — which dependency moved is in package.json, which is in scope.
# This one is load-bearing rather than tidy: node-pty is built from source here
# (see CLAUDE.md), so a dependency bump is a large lockfile diff attached to a
# small, genuinely risky change, and the reviewer's budget should go to the
# second one.
^bun\.lock$
